# First-party image pins — the version of every first-party service prod runs.
#
# TRACKED IN GIT, deliberately (SERV-96). These ten values used to live inside the
# PROD_ENV_FILE environment secret alongside real credentials, which made a version
# change an unlogged secret edit and left promote/rollback (SERV-78, SERV-79) with no
# writable home for them: GITHUB_TOKEN has no `secrets` permission at all, so a
# workflow that repoints a tag by editing the secret needs a PAT with
# environment-secret write — the grant that 403'd in SGNT-29. Editing a tracked file
# needs `contents: write` and nothing else.
#
# The payoff beyond that: a version change is a reviewable diff, and rollback gets its
# index for free — `git log -p versions.env` is the record of what was pinned when.
#
# An image tag is not a credential, and nothing secret belongs in this file. Real
# secrets stay in PROD_ENV_FILE; see .env.example.
#
# ── Convention ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# One variable per SOURCE REPO, not per image: a repo's backend and frontend ship from
# the same release and pin together, so these 10 variables cover 14 images.
#
#   major.minor  services with release-please versions       LYCEUM_TAG=1.10
#                Patch releases still flow in on a `docker compose pull` and nothing
#                else does — the one float kept deliberately, so a security fix lands
#                without an edit here.
#   a commit sha services that publish no semver             ARGOSY_TAG=sha-2f641bd
#                argosy and drydock only, since SERV-89.
#
# The two sha-pinned services do NOT use the same form: argosy's publish workflow tags
# `sha-<short>`, drydock's tags the full 40-char commit sha with no prefix. Copy the
# form the registry actually carries rather than normalising them — a "tidied" tag that
# does not exist resolves to nothing and fails the deploy.
#
# Careful when auditing any of these: a `sha-<short>` tag and a release tag can be
# DIFFERENT images built from the SAME commit, because the publish workflow runs once
# on the push to main and again on the release tag, seconds apart. Compare the
# org.opencontainers.image.revision label, not the digest, or you will invent version
# drift that is not there (SERV-88).
#
# ── How this reaches the stack ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# deploy.yml renders this file and PROD_ENV_FILE into a single $DEPLOY_ROOT/.env via
# scripts/render-env.sh, so the deployed .env stays the complete environment compose
# reads. It then asserts on `docker compose config --images` and fails the deploy if
# any first-party image still resolves to `:latest` or a bare `:`.

APERTURE_TAG=1.14
AMBER_TAG=0.8
ARGOSY_TAG=sha-2f641bd
CENTRIFUGE_TAG=1.5
COOK_BOOK_TAG=1.1
DRYDOCK_TAG=22d5be7fa55a8285a0df50d3976208d48631e8c3
INTERLOCK_TAG=1.3
LYCEUM_TAG=1.10
PURSER_TAG=0.14
SWITCHYARD_TAG=4.13
